Not making an effort to correct flagrant design flaws is, in fact, not
bothering to make the effort to involve people in the rest of the game.

Assuming that "oh, it's hard to do, nobody will do it" is sloppy on the
face of it; it's thinking poorly of your users/players.

>> [...] it is poor design to have useless features in your
>> game... in this case, score.  If pudding farming is removed and scoring
>> is fixed, it will be largely irrelevant to you, (who care not about
>> scores), but will be far more interesting to others who do.
> 
> Nethack as it now stands has a hard limit for max score, which at the
> moment can only be (realistically) reached by certain possibly abusive
> tactics. 

And, by the way, it has been reached at least twice.

> So if score counts, the thing that counts most is getting
> that maximum score. If score is really what people are interested in,
> if you disable pudding farming, and rider farming, and quest farming,
> and gremlin farming, and confused-create-monster acid blob farming,
> and...., people who are motivated by score will just keep trying to
> find other ways to get to that MAXINT number. You'll be chasing after
> them forever.

No software project is completely without flaws, except perhaps "Hello
World"... and you could even make a case for that one.

That doesn't mean that you shouldn't strive to remove the flaws as you
find them, from largest down to smallest.  Eventually the flaws will be
sufficiently small that they are exploitable only in theory.

> If you want a score that says "X is a better player than Y", how are
> you going to do that?

That is a separate issue and requires a lot more discussion and thought;
this thread is not the place for that.  For now, suffice it to say that
the current scoring method is a reasonable indicator of skill for games
that do not end in ascension; it is next to worthless for games that
_do_ end in ascension, however.

I am under no delusions that I will be able to make scoring 'perfect',
but I would like it to be at least somewhat more meaningful.

> No one number will be able to
> capture all of the things that people have as goals in Nethack. This
> is why I don't care much for the highscore list.

I don't agree that it's impossible to capture such a thing in a single
number.  However, I also don't take alternate victory conditions
seriously.  It would be quite sufficient if any scoring method also
successfully tracked the given conducts coded into the game right now.

