> survive / not being willing to tackle the end game without three wands
> of wishing all you want. You can class it as a second-rate ascension,
> one step above explore mode. 

Again, none of this is the point.  The game of vanilla 3.4.3 is what it
is.  If you ascend, you win.  How you ascended doesn't matter unless you
directly cheated in some way -- and I define "cheating" as "using
methods outside the standard mechanics of the game".  The best example
of this is save-scumming.

Pudding farming is, except for the copy/paste bit, entirely contained in
the game mechanics of 3.4.3.  Trying to look down on someone for farming
to an ascension is silly; the goal of the game is to ascend, and that is
one of the means of getting there.

> If I can only survive that way, or if I
> just really like playing that way, why do you, who does not farm, is
> not forced to farm, can win just fine without farming, care so much as
> to remove it?

Because it's flawed design, for reasons stated in this thread, in other
threads, and through numerous threads until the beginning of time, and
because if I want to, say, improve the scoring system, it's a huge
roadblock in my way that says "IT'S A WASTE OF TIME TO INCLUDE THESE
CRITERIA BECAUSE THEY'RE TRIVIALLY BYPASSABLE BY FARMING".  Such
criteria would include # of kills, XP, HP, Pw, gold, # of items, etc.

> Make 'em play the Mac Carbon port :)

A punishment in and of itself.

> If people are skipping past parts of the game, they probably don't
> like those parts of the game: they're too dangerous or too boring or
> whatever. I've read posts here on RGRN from people who *hate* Sokoban.

Correct.  There is a reason I have built a variant, and that is because
I would like to make an effort to make the "boring" parts less so.

To a certain extent, I must do this by closing "bypass" holes and
forcing people to do some things; but there is nothing that says I can't
close the bypass hole and _at the same time_ try to make what's being
bypassed significantly more interesting.

Doing one without the other is ineffective.  But doing both, ah, that's
a trick, isn't it?  And doing both successfully is good design.

> Artifact at the top? Forcing people to do things they dislike just to
> ensure that everyone goes through the same experiences is probably
> contra-productive in the context of a game. 

Of course.  I'm not that thrilled with Sokoban's existence either, since
it's rote memorization and repetition after you solve it once, but ah,
yes, a different topic entirely.  See above paragraph.

> Nethack is a game. It's
> not a sport or a competition or a standardized test. "Shortcuts" are
> fine if you want to use them.

However, it _is_ a competition at least twice a year; the devnull
tournament has been around for a long, long time, and the June NAO
tournament certainly draws a _ton_ of players compared to normal server
operation.

There is enough interest in formalized competition such that taking
competition into consideration in the design is a good thing.

